    <md:title>9.10.6: Vertex Form and Coordinates of the Vertex</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ac2ded2c-6d14-4783-81c7-04c882e24521</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>A quadratic function is defined by the expression \(x^2−20x+19\).</para>
    <para>Rewrite the expression in <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex form (of a quadratic expression)</term> and give the coordinates of the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex (of a graph)</term>. Show your reasoning.</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((x−10)^2−81\). The vertex is at \((10, -81)\).</para>
  <para>\(x^2−20x+19\)
    \(x^2−20x+100+19−100\)
    \(x^2−20x+100−81\)
    \((x−10)^2−81\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
</document>
